<com:TContent ID="body">
	<h1>Building a Simple Currency Converter</h1>
	<p class="block-content">This tutorial introduces the PRADO web application framework and builds a small web application step by step. It assumes that you are familiar with PHP and have access to a web server that serves PHP scripts.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">The application converts a dollar amount to another currency, given the rate of that currency relative to the dollar. The completed application is shown below. <img src="<%~example2.png%>" class="figure" alt="The finished currency converter: a styled form with an exchange rate field, a dollars field, the converted amount, and a Convert button." /> You can try the application <a href="../currency-converter/index.php">locally</a> or at the <a href="https://www.pradoframework.net/demos/currency-converter/">PRADO website</a>. The application works the same when JavaScript is unavailable in the browser.
	</p>

	<h1 id="download">Downloading and Installing Prado</h1>
	<p class="block-content">
	The best way to install PRADO is <a href="https://getcomposer.org/">through composer</a>
	<br/>If you don't use composer yet, first install it:
	</p>
	<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
	curl -s https://getcomposer.org/installer | php
	php composer.phar install
	</com:TTextHighlighter>

	<h1>Creating a new Prado web Application</h1>
	<p class="block-content">
	Then, create the application structure using composer:
	</p>
	<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
	composer create-project pradosoft/prado-app currency-converter
	</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p>
	The command creates a new directory named
	<tt>currency-converter</tt> in your current working directory.
	You may need to change to the appropriate directory
	first.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">The command creates the directory structure and the minimal files, including <tt>index.php</tt> and <tt>Home.page</tt>, of a PRADO web application. Point your browser at the <tt>index.php</tt> script in the <tt>currency-converter</tt> directory. The page shows the message "Welcome to Prado!"
	</p>

	<h1>Creating the Currency Converter User Interface</h1>
	<p class="block-content">Start by editing the <tt>Home.page</tt> file in the <tt>currency-converter/protected/pages/</tt> directory. A file ending in <tt>.page</tt> is a page template that contains HTML and PRADO controls. Add two textboxes, three labels, and one button as follows.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="form1">
&lt;com:TForm&gt;
    <fieldset>
        <legend>Currency Converter</legend>
        <div class="rate-field">
            &lt;com:TLabel ForControl="currencyRate" Text="Exchange Rate per $1:" /&gt;
            &lt;com:TTextBox ID="currencyRate" /&gt;
        </div>
        <div class="dollar-field">
            &lt;com:TLabel ForControl="dollars" Text="Dollars to Convert:" /&gt;
            &lt;com:TTextBox ID="dollars" /&gt;
        </div>
        <div class="total-field">
            <span class="total-label">Amount in Other Currency:</span>
            &lt;com:TLabel ID="total" CssClass="result" /&gt;
        </div>
        <div class="convert-button">
            &lt;com:TButton Text="Convert" /&gt;
        </div>
    </fieldset>
&lt;/com:TForm&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p id="refresh" class="block-content">
	After a refresh, the page looks like the following figure. The layout is unstyled; a later section adds CSS. <img src="<%~example1.png%>" class="figure" alt="The unstyled currency converter form: two labeled text fields, the amount label, and a Convert button." />
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">
		The first component is a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.TForm" Text="TForm" />, which corresponds to the HTML <tt>&lt;form&gt;</tt> element. A PRADO page allows <b>one</b> <tt>TForm</tt>.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">The next two components are a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TLabel" Text="TLabel" /> and a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TTextBox" Text="TTextBox" />. They define a label and a textbox for the currency exchange rate. The <tt>ForControl</tt> property names the component the label is for, so a click on the label focuses the field. A plain HTML <tt>&lt;label&gt;</tt> element needs the rendered <tt>ID</tt> of the textbox, and PRADO renders a component <tt>ID</tt> differently from the template value.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">The next pair of components is similar and defines the textbox for the dollar amount to convert. The <tt>TLabel</tt> with <tt>ID</tt> "total" has no <tt>ForControl</tt> property. It is a plain label that displays the converted amount.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">The final component is a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TButton" Text="TButton" /> that the user clicks to calculate the result. The <tt>Text</tt> property sets the button label.
	</p>

	<h1>Implementing Currency Conversion</h1>

	<p class="block-content">A click on the "Convert" button refreshes the page and does nothing else. For the button to do work, add a <tt>Home.php</tt> file beside <tt>Home.page</tt>. The <tt>Home</tt> class extends <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.TPage" Text="TPage" />, the base class of every PRADO page.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content" id="code3">
&lt;?php
class Home extends TPage
{

}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p class="block-content">
		PRADO autoloads classes on demand, following the convention that a class lives in a file named after the class with the <tt>.php</tt> extension.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">So far the template declares the components and the <tt>Home</tt> class is empty. The next step adds an event handler.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">When the user clicks the "Convert" button, the handler reads the two values, multiplies them, and shows the total. To handle the click, add an <tt>OnClick</tt> property to the "Convert" button in the <tt>Home.page</tt> template and a matching handler method in <tt>Home.php</tt>.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="code4">
&lt;com:TButton Text="Convert" OnClick="convert_clicked" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p class="block-content">
		The <tt>OnClick</tt> value, "<tt>convert_clicked</tt>", names the method in <tt>Home.php</tt> that runs when the user clicks the button.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content" id="code5">
class Home extends TPage
{
    public function convert_clicked($sender, $param)
    {
        $rate = floatval($this->currencyRate->Text);
        $dollars = floatval($this->dollars->Text);
        $this->total->Text = $rate * $dollars;
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<div class="block-content">
	<p>
		Run the application, enter values, and click "Convert". The calculated value appears next to the "Amount in Other Currency" label.
	</p>

	<p>In the <tt>convert_clicked</tt> method, the first parameter, <tt>$sender</tt>, is the object that raised the event, here the "Convert" button. The second parameter, <tt>$param</tt>, carries event-specific data from the sender.
	</p>

	<p>The three lines of <tt>convert_clicked</tt> implement the conversion.
	</p>
</div>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content" id="code6" >
$rate = floatval($this->currencyRate->Text);
</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p class="block-content">
		<tt>$this->currencyRate</tt> is the <tt>TTextBox</tt> with <tt>ID</tt> "currencyRate" in the <tt>Home.page</tt> template. Its <tt>Text</tt> property holds the value the user entered, and <tt>floatval</tt> converts that text to a float.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content" id="code7">
$dollars = floatval($this->dollars->Text);
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<div class="block-content">
	<p>
		The next line reads the <tt>TTextBox</tt> with <tt>ID</tt> "dollars" the same way.
	</p>

	<p>The third line sets the product in the <tt>Text</tt> property of the <tt>TLabel</tt> with <tt>ID="total"</tt>, which displays it.
	</p>
</div>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content" id="code8">
$this->total->Text = $rate * $dollars;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

	<h1>Adding Validation</h1>
	<p class="block-content"><tt>floatval</tt> always yields a number, so the user can enter any text, including letters. Validators tell the user which values the rate and the amount accept.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">For the currency rate, the validators ensure that</p>
		<ol id="o111" class="block-content">
			<li>the user enters a value,</li>
			<li>the currency rate is a valid number,</li>
			<li>the currency rate is positive.</li>
		</ol>
	<p class="block-content">
	 A <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TRequiredFieldValidator" Text="TRequiredFieldValidator" /> covers 1, and a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TCompareValidator" Text="TCompareValidator" /> covers 2 and 3. The validators can sit anywhere in the <tt>Home.page</tt> template. The <a href="?page=Controls.Validation">Validation Controls</a> page describes these and the other validators.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="code9">
&lt;com:TRequiredFieldValidator
	ControlToValidate="currencyRate"
	ErrorMessage="Please enter a currency rate." /&gt;
&lt;com:TCompareValidator
	ControlToValidate="currencyRate"
	DataType="Float"
	ValueToCompare="0"
	Operator="GreaterThan"
	ErrorMessage="Please enter a positive currency rate." /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

	<p class="block-content">For the amount to convert, the validators ensure that</p>
		<ol id="o222" class="block-content">
			<li>the user enters a value,</li>
			<li>the value is a valid number (not including any currency or dollar signs).</li>
		</ol>
	<p class="block-content">
	Another <tt>TRequiredFieldValidator</tt> covers 1, and a <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TDataTypeValidator" Text="TDataTypeValidator" /> covers 2. The application accepts a plain number for the amount.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="code9a">
&lt;com:TRequiredFieldValidator
	ControlToValidate="dollars"
	ErrorMessage="Please enter the amount you wish to calculate." /&gt;
&lt;com:TDataTypeValidator
	ControlToValidate="dollars"
	DataType="Float"
	ErrorMessage="Please enter a number." /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

	<p class="block-content">Invalid or missing input now activates the validators, which show their error messages without reloading the page. By default a validator runs both in JavaScript and on the server, and the server-side validation <b>always runs</b>. The handler skips the calculation when the validators fail:
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content" id="code10" >
public function convert_clicked($sender, $param)
{
    if($this->Page->IsValid)
    {
        $rate = floatval($this->currencyRate->Text);
        $dollars = floatval($this->dollars->Text);
        $this->total->Text = $rate * $dollars;
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

	<h1>Improve User Experience With Active Controls</h1>

	<com:RequiresVersion Version="3.1" />

	<p class="block-content">The application responds faster when it calculates and shows the result without reloading the page.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content"><com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveButton" Text="TActiveButton" />, the active counterpart of <tt>TButton</tt>, raises its server-side click event without a page reload. <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TActiveLabel" Text="TActiveLabel" />, the active counterpart of <tt>TLabel</tt>, lets the server update the "total" label in the browser without a reload.
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="code11">
<div class="total-field">
    <span class="total-label">Amount in Other Currency:</span>
         &lt;com:TActiveLabel ID="total" CssClass="result" /&gt;
    </div>
    <div class="convert-button">
        &lt;com:TActiveButton Text="Convert" OnClick="convert_clicked" /&gt;
</div>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p class="block-content">
		The server-side logic stays the same. PRADO autoloads the active controls on demand, so no import is required.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">The page no longer reloads to show the converted amount. Without a reload, nothing shows that the click did anything. The next step sets the "total" label to "calculating..." when the user clicks "Convert"; the label then receives the calculated amount as before.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">The <tt>ClientSide.OnLoading</tt> property of the "Convert" button, the control that requests the calculation, runs a script when the callback starts:
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="code13">
&lt;com:TActiveButton Text="Convert" OnClick="convert_clicked" &gt;
    &lt;prop:ClientSide.OnLoading&gt;
        $('&lt;%= $this->total->ClientID %&gt;').innerHTML = "calculating..."
    &lt;/prop:ClientSide.OnLoading&gt;
&lt;/com:TActiveButton&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

	<p class="block-content"><tt>ClientSide.OnLoading</tt> and the <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.ActiveControls.TCallbackClientSide" Text="other client-side properties" /> take a JavaScript block as their content or value. <tt>$('...')</tt> returns the element with the given ID, like <tt>document.getElementById('...')</tt>. PRADO renders its own component IDs, so the script uses the rendered ID of the "total" label, <tt>$this->total->ClientID</tt>, inside a <tt>&lt;%= %&gt;</tt> expression. <tt>innerHTML = "calculating..."</tt> replaces the content of the label.
	</p>

	<h1>Adding Final Touches</h1>
	<p class="block-content">The application has no styling yet. A stylesheet can go inline in the template or into a theme.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">A theme is a directory under <tt>themes</tt> in the <tt>currency-converter</tt> directory, and the directory name is the theme name. Create <tt>themes/Basic</tt>, creating <tt>themes</tt> first if needed. See <a href="?page=Advanced.Themes">Themes and Skins</a> for details.
	</p>

	<p class="block-content">Save a CSS file named <tt>common.css</tt> in the <tt>themes/Basic</tt> directory. Then add the following to the beginning of <tt>Home.page</tt>:
	</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content" id="code14">
&lt;%@ Theme="Basic" %&gt;
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
&lt;com:THead Title="Currency Converter"&gt;
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
&lt;/com:THead&gt;
<body>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
	<p class="block-content">
		The first line, <tt>&lt;%@ Theme="Basic" %&gt;</tt>, selects the theme for the page. <com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.THead" Text="THead" /> renders the HTML <tt>&lt;head&gt;</tt> element. It writes the <tt>Title</tt> property and links every CSS file in the <tt>themes/Basic</tt> directory. The finished application looks like the following. <img src="<%~example2.png%>" class="figure" alt="The finished currency converter with the Basic theme applied." /> This completes the tutorial.
	</p>
</com:TContent>
